# The compiler emits LF unconditionally, and `tests/golden_textmap.rs`
# compares its output to these fixtures byte-for-byte. A CRLF checkout on
# Windows fails that comparison — which is exactly what happened on this
# repository's first CI run, on three tests at once.
*.textmap -text
*.json    -text

# WADs are binary. `tests/first_map.rs` compares the committed artifact to
# freshly compiled bytes, so any newline translation would corrupt it.
*.wad binary

# The spec parser is CRLF-tolerant, but its CRLF-identity test transforms
# the `include_str!`'d template with `\n` -> `\r\n` — on a CRLF checkout
# that doubles into `\r\r\n` and the stray `\r` corrupts every line
# (caught by Windows CI on PR #17, same class as the golden pin above).
# Unlike the goldens' `-text` (bytes are sacred there: those artifacts are
# byte-compared and git must never munge them), these are hand-edited
# documents, so `text eol=lf` both checks out LF on every platform AND
# normalizes a future CRLF commit back to LF instead of letting it drift in.
map-spec.template.md text eol=lf
*.spec.md text eol=lf
